{"ts": 1789779126.227097, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The additive/independent model is confirmed cleanly. The sign-flip control (f33 with wrong sign: 0.981 \u2192 0.362) behaves exactly as predicted, and weak-feature triples stay near zero. There is no interaction structure anywhere in this panel.\n\nThis settles the key question. My best, {2, 10, -33} at 0.981, is the top of the additive ordering \u2014 it uses the three largest |Sharpe| features, and no combination can beat \u221a(\u03a3s\u1d62\u00b2) over the three best singles. Continuing to search would only mean grinding through hundreds of remaining triples hoping for an in-sample fluctuation to push past the 1.026 bar. That is precisely the wrong move: with ~9880 possible sign-free triples, a 0.05 overshoot is easy to find by chance and would be pure selection noise that doesn't replicate. I'll stop searching and submit my honest best rather than manufacture a bar-clearing number.\n\nNow the forecast, which should be about OOS reality, not the in-sample figure.\n\nTwo corrections to 0.981 are warranted:\n\n**Selection bias.** I chose {2,10,33} as the max over my search. Even though the additive model says this is genuinely the right triple, the *singles* I ranked on were themselves noisy. With n=5000, the standard error on a Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/5000) annualized \u2248 0.19 in daily-Sharpe terms scaled up \u2014 concretely, picking the top 3 of 40 features induces meaningful upward bias in their estimated strengths. The true s\u1d62 are likely somewhat below 0.589, 0.580, 0.544.\n\n**Sign selection.** I also fit 3 signs in-sample. That's minor here since the signs are unambiguous (all far from zero), so it adds little.\n\nQuantifying the shrinkage: across 40 features the observed |Sharpe| distribution has a bulk around 0.1\u20130.25 with a handful near 0.5\u20130.6. That pattern suggests the top few are real signal, not noise \u2014 a pure-noise panel at n=5000 would rarely throw up three features at 0.54+. So I expect genuine but modestly overstated signal. Shrinking each single by ~15% gives \u221a(0.50\u00b2+0.49\u00b2+0.46\u00b2) \u2248 0.84.\n\nI'll center near 0.84 and widen for the possibility that the features are less stationary OOS than in-sample."}
