{"ts": 1789779170.155288, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** `+f2 +f10 \u2212f33`, in-sample Sharpe **0.981**. Predicted OOS: **0.82 \u00b1 0.28**.\n\n### What the data turned out to be\n\nI scanned all 40 singles, then pairs and triples among the strongest. The panel has a very simple structure: **the features are essentially independent signal carriers with no interactions.** Every combination's Sharpe is predicted by \u221a(\u03a3s\u1d62\u00b2) over its components:\n\n| Spec | Predicted (additive) | Observed |\n|---|---|---|\n| {2,10,\u221233} | 0.99 | 0.981 |\n| {2,10,\u22126} | 0.97 | 0.959 |\n| {2,10,29} | 0.97 | 0.954 |\n\nI ran two controls to make sure this wasn't an artifact. Flipping f33's sign collapsed the triple from 0.981 \u2192 0.362, and triples of weak features (`{0,7,31}`, `{5,12,39}`) came in at \u22120.02 and 0.07. Both behaved exactly as the model predicts.\n\n### Why I stopped at 0.981 against a 1.026 bar\n\nBecause the structure is purely additive, `{2,10,\u221233}` is provably the top spec \u2014 it takes the three largest |Sharpe| features, and nothing can exceed \u221a(\u03a3s\u1d62\u00b2) over the best three. There is no hidden interaction left to find.\n\nThat means the only way to clear the bar was to keep grinding through the ~9880 remaining triples until one overshot. With that many candidates, a 0.05 overshoot is trivially easy to find by chance, and it would be **selection noise that doesn't replicate out of sample**. Clearing the bar that way would have produced a worse specification and a dishonest forecast. The bar is search-adjusted precisely to penalize this, so I stopped and reported my genuine best.\n\n### The forecast is below the in-sample number, deliberately\n\nI centered at 0.82 rather than 0.981 because I selected these three features by ranking on noisy estimates. At n=5000 the top-of-40 estimates are biased upward, so the true s\u1d62 are likely below the observed 0.589/0.580/0.544. Shrinking ~15% gives \u221a(0.50\u00b2+0.49\u00b2+0.46\u00b2) \u2248 0.84.\n\nI do think the signal is **real** \u2014 a pure-noise panel at this sample size would rarely produce three features at 0.54+, and the additive structure held across every combination I tested. So I expect genuine decay from selection bias, not collapse. The sd of 0.28 covers the shrinkage uncertainty plus the risk that these relationships are less stationary out of sample than in.\n\nOne housekeeping note: several claude.ai MCP connectors (Gmail, Drive, Linear, etc.) reported needing OAuth authorization. None were relevant here, but they'd need authorizing via claude.ai connector settings or `claude mcp` in an interactive session if you want them available later."}
